A||||8||Fret Naught||Oil Leak||||7||
Z||000000||Fret Naught||01-14-2001||12:05 PM||||I had my car up on a hoist the other day, When ever I do this, I make it a point to take a look at the underside just to see how things are doing. I have an oil leak that I would bet is a rear main seal at least that is what I would call it. It is comeing from where the engine and the transmissing join.<BR>Any thoughts? Since I have never had one of these apart before, I am not 100% sure. I assume the engine and tranny need to come out an be seperated to replace it. here hoping I am wrong!!! thanks in advance<BR>Fret Naught<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FretNaught_sig_18.gif"> ||||reg||7
Z||000001||theogre||01-14-2001||12:19 PM||||You have to be sure wether the oil is comming form the engine or tranny there. With a manual tranny you can't go by texture or color as easily.<P>In other words... could be the rear main ot the input seal on the tranny. could be oil pan to.<P>Is the oil coming out of the bell housing or is the stone shield wet on the engine side?<P>try taking off the stone shield. If it's the rear main there is often oil tracked on the flywheel.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||01-14-2001||12:21 PM||||I've had a rear main oil seal leak on my Formula ever since I bought it. It just leaves a few drips overnight, nothing major or noticeable. I also have an intake oil leak that leaks down from the top of the block. I say if it doesn't leak too badly, just leave it be. You would need to sepatate the tranny from the engine, remove the flywheel, remove the oil pan(maybe), and probably a few other things. It's not worth it to me to stop a few drips, but that's me.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||01-14-2001||01:19 PM||||I would also suggest you check for an oil leak at the base of your distributor (V6 engine). A bad distributor O-ring can result in a similar condition, since the oil tends to run down and drip off the lowest thing, which is the bell housing.  If that's the case, consider yourself very lucky since this is an easy fix.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||01-14-2001||02:15 PM||||I second Mark's comment.  Unless you did it last week, pull the distributor, replace the o-ring, pickup coil, etc. ||||reg||14
Z||000005||fiero56||01-14-2001||02:31 PM||||You guys talking to me? <P>I replaced the dist o-ring the day after I got it. Didn't fix the leak up top. It leaks out between the block and lower intake manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||01-14-2001||02:34 PM||||had same prob last year. looked for sure like rear main leaking, was just O ring on distributor. Not a drop leaking since. it just runs down intake and block right to where rear main is.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||01-14-2001||08:42 PM||||Thanks guys,<BR>I suspected the dis. O Ring, but looked around the base real close with an inspection light no Oil around it? wouldn't I see some there if that were the cause?<P>If it is the rear main, I will just live with it till I pull the engine, I just have this thing about oil leaks, I HATE EM!!!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FretNaught_sig_19.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000008||Carrolles||01-15-2001||02:00 PM||||Another possibility is the rear cam shaft cover.  It uses RTV instead of a gasket and will sometimes cause a leak that you discribe.  To replace it you'll need to separate the engine from the trans and remove the flywheel.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
